      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "This study aims to conduct a meta-analysis focusing on the associations between psychological constructs and sport performance, as these relationships have been widely examined in existing literature [12,13].\n\nThe hypotheses of the study are formulated as follows: Hypothesis 1: There is an association between psychological factors including motivation, self-efficacy, self-confidence, goal setting, attention, stress management, anxiety, openness to experience, neuroticism, agreeableness, extraversion, conscientiousness, personality traits, and emotional intelligence (EI) and sport performance. Hypothesis 2: Gender (male/female), athlete type (Elite/Non-elite), and sport type (Team/Individual) moderate the association between psychological factors and sport performance.",
      "reason": "The introduction explicitly states the review aim (meta-analysis of associations between psychological constructs and sport performance) and lists two formal hypotheses specifying the questions and planned moderators, satisfying the requirement to state objectives/questions."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "This study aims to conduct a meta-analysis focusing on the associations between psychological constructs and sport performance, as these relationships have been widely examined in existing literature [12,13].",
      "reason": "The review examines associations (observational/correlational studies), not intervention effects; therefore PICO-style planned comparisons for interventions do not apply."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "During the search process, titles, abstracts, and keywords were screened based on the following inclusion criteria: (a) the study employed quantitative methods; (b) it examined the relationship between psychological factors such as motivation, self-efficacy, self-confidence, goal setting, attention, stress management, anxiety, openness to experience, neuroticism, agreeableness, extraversion, conscientiousness, personality, and emotional intelligence (EI) and sports performance; (c) the sample consisted of participants involved in sports competitions; and (d) the study presented an effect size or a correlational analysis.",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies several study-level eligibility characteristics (study design limited to quantitative methods, specific psychological factors, participant setting as athletes in competitions, and requirement of effect size/correlational analysis) but does not fully report other study-characteristic items requested by the criterion (e.g., broader eligible study designs beyond 'quantitative', specific settings details, follow-up requirements, or a formal question-framework specification)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": \"The searches were conducted in the Web of Science, Scopus, and SPORTDiscuss databases, covering articles published from January 2014 to August 1, 2024.\",
      "reason": "The date range for eligible reports is explicitly provided (2014–Aug 1, 2024), but the evidence does not specify other report-characteristic restrictions such as language limits or publication/report status (e.g., grey literature inclusion/exclusion), so this requirement is only partially addressed."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": \"After applying the JBI quality assessment, 10 studies were excluded from the analysis as they did not meet the necessary inclusion criteria. Reasons for exclusion included reporting data solely as descriptive statistics, failure to provide sample size (n), and insufficient methodological detail to allow for meaningful statistical analysis.\",
      "reason": "The manuscript and PRISMA flow diagram explicitly list and distinguish exclusion reasons that relate to outcomes/data not being reported (e.g., only descriptive statistics, missing sample size, insufficient methodological detail) versus studies not including the relevant performance values, indicating the review differentiates exclusions due to outcomes not being measured from exclusions due to results not being reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": \"The descriptive findings of the studies were coded into three groups based on gender, type of sport, and athlete level... For the examination of publications, the studies were divided into two groups based on publication years to ensure a homogeneous distribution: 2014-2019 and 2020-2024.\",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies how studies were grouped for syntheses (gender, sport type, athlete level, and publication-year groups) and later reports subgroup analyses (e.g., gender, athlete type, sport type), linking groupings to the synthesis/comparisons."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": \"The primary aim of our study was to identify the psychological factors affecting sports performance and to analyze the interplay between sports performance using quantitative methods.\",
      "reason": "The authors provide a rationale for at least one notable restriction (limiting to quantitative methods) tied to the study aim, but they do not provide explicit rationales for other notable restrictions (e.g., the chosen publication year range or any language/publication-status restrictions), so the justification for restrictions is only partially provided."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "70"
}

